1
00:00:05,104 --> 00:00:05,662
你好

2
00:00:07,959 --> 00:00:09,741
I'm Yu Takahashi from Japan.

3
00:00:09,766 --> 00:00:13,558
Today I'll talk about macros 
in SwiftUI Preview.

4
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:17,404
Let's start with what I wanted to build.

5
00:00:17,429 --> 00:00:20,350
I usually use SwiftData
for my indie apps,

6
00:00:20,781 --> 00:00:23,559
but when we use Previews for SwiftData,

7
00:00:23,583 --> 00:00:27,111
we need to add a model container
and more, like this.

8
00:00:28,896 --> 00:00:31,368
And manually insert mock data.

9
00:00:31,393 --> 00:00:36,600
But I wanted to simplify this
with my custom macro,

10
00:00:36,812 --> 00:00:39,674
like this PreviewContent macro.

11
00:00:39,949 --> 00:00:46,968
This macro generates a new struct
that takes mock values as parameters.

12
00:00:47,539 --> 00:00:49,500
It looks very nice, right?

13
00:00:49,708 --> 00:00:51,670
However, I faced an error.

14
00:00:53,739 --> 00:00:57,829
Compiler can't find the struct
that the macro generates.

15
00:00:58,310 --> 00:00:59,983
It seems like a bug.

16
00:01:00,549 --> 00:01:05,162
But what if I write the struct manually?

17
00:01:05,343 --> 00:01:09,488
Of course, the error is gone.

18
00:01:09,554 --> 00:01:11,354
Now it can be previewed.

19
00:01:13,199 --> 00:01:17,430
So I looked into it.
It turns out I'm not the only one.

20
00:01:18,378 --> 00:01:23,990
There's a thread on the Swift forums
reporting the exact same symptom.

21
00:01:25,029 --> 00:01:28,485
The access method generated by
the Observable macro

22
00:01:28,517 --> 00:01:32,450
cannot be found in the Preview macro.

23
00:01:33,519 --> 00:01:36,180
I'll come back to this later.

24
00:01:37,429 --> 00:01:40,897
Before going deeper,
let me describe Swift macros.

25
00:01:41,338 --> 00:01:43,640
There are two types of macros.

26
00:01:43,682 --> 00:01:47,442
freestanding macros and attached macros.

27
00:01:49,476 --> 00:01:52,350
Also, there are many types
of attached macros,

28
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:54,485
like peer, member, and more.

29
00:01:55,449 --> 00:01:57,864
The main characters in this topic

30
00:01:57,889 --> 00:02:01,184
are the freestanding macro
and the peer-attached macro.

31
00:02:04,709 --> 00:02:10,102
My custom PreviewContent macro
is a peer-attached macro,

32
00:02:10,222 --> 00:02:11,711
and it creates a new struct.

33
00:02:12,576 --> 00:02:17,397
SwiftUI's Preview macro
is a freestanding macro.

34
00:02:18,509 --> 00:02:24,896
And the reference to the generated struct
is inside its argument.

35
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:26,752
Here's the problem.

36
00:02:28,439 --> 00:02:33,825
According to the proposal, macros
are expanded in two separate phases.

37
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:38,093
First, the compiler type-checks
the macro's arguments

38
00:02:38,118 --> 00:02:39,883
without expanding the macro.

39
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:46,425
Second, the compiler expands the macro
and type-checks the result.

40
00:02:48,810 --> 00:02:52,543
The body of the Preview macro
is type-checked in the first phase.

41
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:58,214
However, the generated struct is expanded
and type-checked in the second phase.

42
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:01,635
And now the reason is clear, right?

43
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:06,482
When the compiler type-checks
the Preview macro's arguments,

44
00:03:06,589 --> 00:03:09,650
the generated struct isn't there yet.

45
00:03:11,674 --> 00:03:13,681
Let's go back to the forum example.

46
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:19,210
Both the foo() and access() methods
come from the Observable macro.

47
00:03:19,940 --> 00:03:23,050
But only foo() resolves. Why?

48
00:03:23,750 --> 00:03:26,300
The foo() method is part
of the Observable protocol.

49
00:03:26,815 --> 00:03:31,332
As we can see at the top,
the attached Observable macro

50
00:03:31,357 --> 00:03:34,516
declares conformance
to the Observable protocol.

51
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:39,785
So the model class conforms
to the Observable protocol.

52
00:03:40,457 --> 00:03:45,655
This is obvious
without expanding the macro.

53
00:03:46,668 --> 00:03:49,812
So the Observable protocol,
including the foo() method,

54
00:03:49,841 --> 00:03:55,201
is visible in the Preview macro.

55
00:03:56,690 --> 00:03:59,063
However, the access() method is different.

56
00:03:59,551 --> 00:04:03,817
access() is a member which
the Observable macro generates

57
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:08,333
inside the model class,
after macro expansion.

58
00:04:09,207 --> 00:04:14,030
This is exactly the kind of problem
I just said earlier.

59
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:17,370
So, is this a bug?

60
00:04:18,452 --> 00:04:20,048
No, this is the design.

61
00:04:20,669 --> 00:04:24,735
The proposal clearly says:
keep macro-generated names

62
00:04:24,778 --> 00:04:28,803
invisible in other macros' arguments.

63
00:04:29,432 --> 00:04:34,222
However, this behavior isn't mentioned
in any of the documents shown.

64
00:04:35,921 --> 00:04:41,650
There are issues
if names can be visible on other macros:

65
00:04:42,289 --> 00:04:45,903
self-validation of arguments,
order dependency,

66
00:04:45,954 --> 00:04:51,136
which Swift doesn't prefer,
and expansion cost.

67
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:58,352
The alternative way to preview
using my custom PreviewContent macro

68
00:04:58,377 --> 00:05:03,231
is to use the old PreviewProvider struct.

69
00:05:04,090 --> 00:05:07,283
Because this is just a plain struct,
it'll be type-checked

70
00:05:07,334 --> 00:05:09,841
at the same time as
the attached macro expansion.

71
00:05:11,130 --> 00:05:16,251
Using the old API feels wrong,
but today this is the only answer.

72
00:05:17,252 --> 00:05:17,990
Thank you.

